The 23.3s wall saving EXCEEDS the 16.0s of re-parse removed, because dropping
three of four walks also removes their file reads and AST traversals -- the
parse timer only ever saw part of the cost. _text_filters.py:234 fell 3380 ->
1139 calls (5.2s -> 1.6s) for the same reason.

WHAT MAKES SHARING POSSIBLE: the file-walking half of both scans is a pure
function of the files on disk; only the RESOLUTION half (handler name -> Symbol,
class -> its methods) consults the symbol table, and the two callers hold
different symbol lists. _CliScanFacts carries the symbol-free facts.

THE MEMO IS SINGLE-SLOT AND CONTENT-KEYED, deliberately. Single slot because the
access pattern is "same root, four times in a row", and a growing dict would
leak across a long test run. Keyed on path+size+mtime of every file rather than
on root alone, because a root-only key is a STALE-HIT generator the moment
anything edits a file between two scans -- the same failure mode WI-madut names
for persistent per-file memoization. A survey never mutates its own tree
mid-run; test suites do constantly. The residual hole (same-size edit inside one
mtime tick) is covered by _reset_cli_scan_cache for tests needing certainty.
test_rescans_after_a_file_changes is the load-bearing test here: every
equivalence test in the file would pass against a cache that never invalidates.

ONE REGRESSION CAUGHT WHILE WRITING THE MERGE, not after. The fire probe
originally ran over EVERY ast.Call node; the argparse probe runs over an
if/elif in which a Call that is an Assign's VALUE takes the first branch.
Folding the fire probe into that elif -- the obvious way to merge two loops --
silently stops matching `cli = fire.Fire(Service)`, and no existing test used
that shape. The fire probe now sits outside the if/elif and
test_fire_target_assigned_to_a_variable_is_still_found pins it.

Scope note: this is the STRAIGHTFORWARD part of the analyzer-phase target. The
